    {
      "id": "dNsupOO6m7tbyod9YUFVP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2192.997767857143,
      "y": 114.55022321428639,
      "width": 8076.01416015625,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1213431527,
      "version": 19,
      "versionNonce": 1039349671,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777225697309,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Delegate, don’t overload: Instead of making one agent responsible for everything, MACRS delegates roles to specialized responders. This mirrors how human teams work, task division leads to improved focus, clearer logic, and easier debugging.\n\n- Strategize with oversight: The planner agent isn’t just a traffic cop, it’s a strategist. Its job is to reason across different options and select the one that advances the goal. This kind of centralized planning, fed by distributed agents, is a powerful design pattern when your system needs to adapt dynamically while staying on course.\n\n- Maintain shared memory: By giving all agents access to a common user profile and dialogue history, MACRS ensures coherence. Every response, whether it’s a question, a suggestion, or small talk, is informed by what’s already known. This creates conversations that feel intelligent, not disjointed. Specifically, the memory modules for both responder and planner agents contain a combination of dialogue history, user profile, strategy-level suggestions, dialogue act history, and corrective experiences. This shared context is crucial for informed decision-making across the multi-agent system.\n\n- Orchestrate, don’t dominate: Even though the planner agent is in charge of the final output, it doesn’t micromanage the responders. It lets them propose ideas freely based on their domain expertise. This kind of loose coupling with tight coordination is what makes MACRS both flexible and goal-aligned.\n\n- Trade-offs of multi-agent orchestration (latency and cost): While MACRS’s multi-agent architecture offers significant advantages in dialogue control and adaptability, it’s important for system designers to recognize a practical trade-off. For each turn in the conversation, this architecture typically involves four separate LLM calls (one for each of the three responder agents and one for the planner agent). This design choice, while enabling sophisticated planning and specialization, can introduce increased latency and operational costs compared to a single, monolithic LLM call. However, this overhead is often justified by the enhanced performance in goal-directed dialogue, and improved interpretability. It also leads to greater modularity for development and maintenance, as demonstrated by MACRS’s superior results.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Delegate, don’t overload: Instead of making one agent responsible for everything, MACRS delegates roles to specialized responders. This mirrors how human teams work, task division leads to improved focus, clearer logic, and easier debugging.\n\n- Strategize with oversight: The planner agent isn’t just a traffic cop, it’s a strategist. Its job is to reason across different options and select the one that advances the goal. This kind of centralized planning, fed by distributed agents, is a powerful design pattern when your system needs to adapt dynamically while staying on course.\n\n- Maintain shared memory: By giving all agents access to a common user profile and dialogue history, MACRS ensures coherence. Every response, whether it’s a question, a suggestion, or small talk, is informed by what’s already known. This creates conversations that feel intelligent, not disjointed. Specifically, the memory modules for both responder and planner agents contain a combination of dialogue history, user profile, strategy-level suggestions, dialogue act history, and corrective experiences. This shared context is crucial for informed decision-making across the multi-agent system.\n\n- Orchestrate, don’t dominate: Even though the planner agent is in charge of the final output, it doesn’t micromanage the responders. It lets them propose ideas freely based on their domain expertise. This kind of loose coupling with tight coordination is what makes MACRS both flexible and goal-aligned.\n\n- Trade-offs of multi-agent orchestration (latency and cost): While MACRS’s multi-agent architecture offers significant advantages in dialogue control and adaptability, it’s important for system designers to recognize a practical trade-off. For each turn in the conversation, this architecture typically involves four separate LLM calls (one for each of the three responder agents and one for the planner agent). This design choice, while enabling sophisticated planning and specialization, can introduce increased latency and operational costs compared to a single, monolithic LLM call. However, this overhead is often justified by the enhanced performance in goal-directed dialogue, and improved interpretability. It also leads to greater modularity for development and maintenance, as demonstrated by MACRS’s superior results.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "bU6JWs5LRMiOeKOnTej0Y",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2198.857142857143,
